.Dd October 19, 2026
.Dt KAIGI.CONF 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kaigi.conf
.Nd Kaigi deployment configuration
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The file
.Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/kaigi.conf
contains non-secret deployment, site, network, localization and presentation
settings for the Kaigi service on FreeBSD.
Kaigi reads it during startup;
it is not sourced by
.Xr rc 8
and is not shell code.
.Pp
Each setting is written as
.Dq NAME=VALUE .
Leading and trailing whitespace around names and values is ignored.
Blank lines and lines beginning with
.Ql #
are ignored.
Matching single or double quotes surrounding an entire value are optional and
are removed.
The first
.Ql =
separates the name from the value.
Inline comments are not supported.
.Pp
The sample deliberately leaves optional settings commented out.
An explicitly supplied application environment variable has highest priority,
followed by the corresponding setting in this file, with Kaigi's defaults
as the final fallback.
Language-specific site text is configured separately as described below.
.Sh SITE TEXT TRANSLATIONS
User-facing site text examples are provided in
.Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/site-translations.json.sample .
The installed
.Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/site-translations.json
is administrator-owned configuration and is preserved by package upgrades.
It is read at startup; restart the service after editing it.
.Pp
The JSON object is keyed by language code (for example en, en-CA, fr-CA, ja).
Each language object maps a supported setting name to a string:
SITE_NAME, SITE_DESCRIPTION, SITE_COPYRIGHT, PAGE_TITLE,
JOIN_DESCRIPTION, JOIN_BUTTON_LABEL, CUSTOMIZE_BUTTON_LABEL, JOIN_LAST_LABEL,
NEW_ROOM_TITLE, NEW_ROOM_DESCRIPTION, LOGIN_HEADING, LOGIN_INSTRUCTIONS,
and LOGIN_BUTTON_LABEL.
URLs, email addresses and asset paths remain in kaigi.conf.
.Pp
Browser text uses an exact language entry, then its base language, then en,
then the existing deployment/environment or application default value.
Configure language-specific text in site-translations.json.
If no translation is available, the configured text or application default is used.
The selected language follows UI_LANGUAGE and the permitted UI_LANGUAGES menu.
An empty translation is an explicit override, not a missing entry.
The file must contain valid JSON without comments or variable expansion.
Unknown text-setting names and invalid value types stop startup with an error.
The translation contents are public; never put passwords, tokens or other
secrets in this file.
.Sh SECRET FILES
.Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/users
contains local-user passwords and meeting grants; keep it root:wheel mode 0600.
.Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/rc.d/kaigi
reads it as root and exports HOST_USERS before starting the service user.
.Pp
The rc script creates
.Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/jwt-secret
if absent, with 32 random bytes encoded as 64 hexadecimal characters and
umask 077. It reuses the file on subsequent starts and exports JWT_SECRET;
it rejects an empty file and does not automatically rotate an existing secret.
The daemon requires at least 32 characters and rejects the published default.
Independent authentication, recording-upload and RTMP keys are derived from
this master secret; token verification accepts only HS256.
Keep the file root-owned and private. Changing it invalidates outstanding
signed tokens after a service restart.
.Pp
LDAP_BIND_PASSWORD_FILE in ldap/ldap.conf identifies a different secret:
the administrator-managed LDAP search-account password, read directly by
the daemon. See kaigi-ldap.conf(5) for permissions.
WEB_SSL_KEY identifies a TLS private key, which must also be protected.
.Sh VARIABLE EXPANSION
A value may reference another variable defined in this file as
.Dq ${VARIABLE} .
References may point forward or backward and may be nested.
For example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
WEB_LISTEN_PORT=3010
WEB_URL=https://meet.example.com:${WEB_LISTEN_PORT}
.Ed
.Pp
Undefined and circular references are configuration errors.
Only variables in
.Nm
participate in expansion; process environment variables are not consulted
while expanding values.
.Sh SITE IDENTITY
These settings identify the particular hosted service while preserving
upstream MiroTalk attribution.
.Bl -tag -width "SITE_CONTACT_EMAIL"
.It Ev SITE_NAME
Site or service name.
.It Ev SITE_DESCRIPTION
Short site description.
.It Ev SITE_URL
Public site URL.
.It Ev SITE_CONTACT_EMAIL
Site contact email address.
.It Ev SITE_COPYRIGHT
Optional copyright notice for site-owned material such as logos, artwork,
site-authored text, policies, and other site-specific content.
If omitted, no site copyright notice is displayed.
MiroTalk software copyright and licensing information remains on the local
About page.
.It Ev SITE_LOGO_NAME
Logo file name relative to
.Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/site-assets .
.It Ev SITE_LOGO_URL
Optional target URL for the clickable logo; normally the same as
.Ev SITE_URL .
.El
.Pp
Files in
.Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/site-assets
are served under
.Pa /site-assets/ .
Asset settings ending in
.Dq _NAME
contain names relative to that directory, not URL paths.
The administrator stylesheet
.Pa site.css
is loaded after Kaigi's packaged CSS.
.Sh PRESENTATION OVERRIDES
These settings override selected high-visibility UI strings.
Leave them absent to retain Kaigi's defaults and localization.
They may intentionally contain bilingual text.
.Bl -tag -width "CUSTOMIZE_BUTTON_LABEL"
.It Ev PAGE_TITLE
Browser page-title override.
.It Ev SITE_ICON_NAME
Favicon file name in the site-assets directory.
.It Ev APPLE_TOUCH_ICON_NAME
Apple touch-icon file name in the site-assets directory.
.It Ev ABOUT_IMAGE_NAME
About-page/dialog image file name in the site-assets directory.
Kaigi maps this setting to
.Ev ABOUT_IMAGE_URL
as
.Pa /site-assets/<name> .
If omitted, the configured site logo is used, followed by the packaged
MiroTalk logo.
.It Ev JOIN_DESCRIPTION
Join-room description.
.It Ev JOIN_BUTTON_LABEL
Meeting-join button label.
.It Ev CUSTOMIZE_BUTTON_LABEL
Customize-room button label.
.It Ev JOIN_LAST_LABEL
Recent-room label.
.It Ev NEW_ROOM_TITLE
Meeting-selection title displayed after login.
.It Ev NEW_ROOM_DESCRIPTION
Description below the meeting-selection title after login.
.It Ev LOGIN_HEADING
Login-page heading.
.It Ev LOGIN_INSTRUCTIONS
Instructions displayed on the login panel.
.It Ev LOGIN_BUTTON_LABEL
Login button label.
.It Ev SHOW_SHARE_ROOM_POPUP
Set to
.Dq false
to suppress the automatic invitation dialog while retaining the invitation command.
Invitations show the site URL and meeting name separately; QR codes contain
only the site URL.
.El
.Sh LOCALIZATION
Kaigi uses bundled language files for general interface localization.
Site text in site-translations.json follows the selected UI language.
General interface labels use the application's local language files.
.Bl -tag -width "UI_LANGUAGE"
.It Ev UI_LANGUAGE
Interface language override.  The default is
.Li en .
.It Ev UI_LANGUAGES
Optional comma-separated language codes to offer in the local language menus,
in menu order (for example,
.Li ja,fr-CA ) .
If unset or empty, all bundled languages are offered.
Language codes are case-sensitive.  An unsupported code in either setting,
or an empty entry in a nonempty
.Ev UI_LANGUAGES
list, causes the service to fail at startup with an error naming the
invalid code and listing the supported codes.
The default
.Ev UI_LANGUAGE
should be included when restricting the menu; otherwise the first listed
language becomes the initial selection.
.El
Supported language codes are:
.Bl -column "fr-CA" "French (Canada)" -offset indent
.It Sy Code Ta Sy Language
.It Li en Ta English
.It Li en-CA Ta English (Canada)
.It Li hu Ta Hungarian
.It Li es Ta Spanish
.It Li fr Ta French
.It Li fr-CA Ta French (Canada)
.It Li de Ta German
.It Li pt Ta Portuguese
.It Li it Ta Italian
.It Li ru Ta Russian
.It Li zh Ta Chinese
.It Li ja Ta Japanese
.It Li ar Ta Arabic
.It Li hi Ta Hindi
.It Li sr Ta Serbian
.It Li id Ta Indonesian
.It Li ko Ta Korean
.It Li tr Ta Turkish
.It Li nl Ta Dutch
.El
.Sh MEETING ENTRY
Every participant must authenticate and have permission to join the selected
meeting. Any authorized participant can start an empty meeting.
Kaigi has no presenter or host role, lobby, or action that ends a meeting
for all attendees. Participants cannot control another participant's media
or admission. See
.Pa %%DOCSDIR%%/HARDENING.md
for the security model.
.Sh AUTHENTICATED SESSION LIFETIME
These settings control authenticated session lifetime in Kaigi.
Values are expressed in seconds.
.Bl -tag -width "AUTH_SESSION_MAX_AGE"
.It Ev AUTH_IDLE_TIMEOUT
Maximum permitted inactivity before the authenticated session is terminated.
Meaningful browser activity refreshes this timeout while the user is on the
room-selection or pre-join pages.
While the user is connected to an active meeting, the authenticated browser
periodically refreshes the session so a quiet participant is not logged out
merely for not using the keyboard or mouse.
Default: 1800 seconds.
Set to 0 to disable the inactivity limit.
.It Ev AUTH_SESSION_MAX_AGE
Absolute maximum lifetime of an authenticated session.
This limit is measured from successful login and is never extended by browser
activity or active meeting participation.
When the limit expires, the authentication token and server-side authorization
are invalidated and the browser is returned to the login page.
Default: 28800 seconds.
Set to 0 to disable the absolute session-age limit.
.El
.Pp
On an idle or absolute timeout, Kaigi clears the session-scoped browser
authentication state and revokes the corresponding server-side authorization.
.Sh LISTENER AND MEDIA CONFIGURATION
.Bl -tag -width "MEDIA_ANNOUNCED_HOST"
.It Ev WEB_LISTEN_HOST
Externally meaningful hostname of the Kaigi service.
When
.Ev WEB_LISTEN_IP
is omitted, this hostname is resolved through the system resolver and must
match exactly one IPv4 address assigned to the local host.
.It Ev WEB_LISTEN_IP
Optional local bind address.
Set this explicitly for NAT, reverse-proxy, jail or other deployments where
the public hostname resolves to an address not assigned directly to the host.
.It Ev WEB_LISTEN_PORT
Local listen port.
Default: 3010.
.It Ev WEB_URL
Canonical public URL of the Kaigi conferencing service.
When omitted and both
.Ev WEB_LISTEN_HOST
and
.Ev WEB_LISTEN_PORT
are configured, Kaigi derives an HTTPS URL from that hostname and port.
Set it explicitly when a reverse proxy or port mapping makes that URL
inappropriate.
.It Ev WEB_SSL_CERT
TLS certificate pathname.
Absolute pathnames are supported.
.It Ev WEB_SSL_KEY
TLS private-key pathname.
Absolute pathnames are supported.
.El
.Pp
.Ev SITE_URL
and
.Ev WEB_URL
serve different purposes.
.Ev SITE_URL
identifies the organization or site associated with the service and is the
normal destination of the site logo.
.Ev WEB_URL
identifies the conferencing service itself.
For example, they may respectively be
.Dq https://www.example.com/
and
.Dq https://meet.example.com/ .
.Sh WEBRTC MEDIA
These settings control mediasoup's WebRTC media sockets and are independent
of the web/signaling listener.
.Bl -tag -width "MEDIA_ANNOUNCED_HOST"
.It Ev MEDIA_LISTEN_IP
Optional local media bind address.
When omitted it defaults to the effective
.Ev WEB_LISTEN_IP .
.It Ev MEDIA_ANNOUNCED_HOST
Optional hostname whose IPv4 address is announced to remote WebRTC peers.
The hostname is resolved through the system resolver when Kaigi starts.
.It Ev MEDIA_ANNOUNCED_IP
Optional literal IPv4 address announced to remote WebRTC peers.
.Ev MEDIA_ANNOUNCED_HOST
and
.Ev MEDIA_ANNOUNCED_IP
are mutually exclusive.
When neither is configured,
.Ev WEB_LISTEN_HOST
is resolved and used as the announced media address.
If the announced hostname resolves to more than one IPv4 address, startup
fails and the administrator must set
.Ev MEDIA_ANNOUNCED_IP
explicitly.
.It Ev MEDIA_MIN_PORT
First media port.
Default: 40000.
.It Ev MEDIA_MAX_PORT
Last media port.
Default: 40100.
.It Ev MEDIA_NUM_WORKERS
Optional mediasoup worker count.
When omitted, Kaigi derives the worker count from the available CPUs.
.El
.Pp
The FreeBSD port does not contact public IP-discovery services.
Administrators control addressing through DNS and the settings above.
.Pp
For a directly addressed host, a typical configuration needs only:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
WEB_LISTEN_HOST=meet.example.com
WEB_LISTEN_PORT=3010
.Ed
.Pp
For a NAT deployment where public DNS does not resolve to an address assigned
to the Kaigi host, specify the local bind address:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
WEB_LISTEN_HOST=meet.example.com
WEB_LISTEN_IP=192.168.1.20
.Ed
.Pp
For a reverse proxy, the service URL may differ from the local listener:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
WEB_LISTEN_HOST=meet.example.com
WEB_LISTEN_IP=127.0.0.1
WEB_LISTEN_PORT=3010
WEB_URL=https://meet.example.com/
.Ed
.Pp
For a separate media network, override the media settings independently:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
MEDIA_LISTEN_IP=192.168.2.20
MEDIA_ANNOUNCED_HOST=media.example.com
.Ed
.Pp
The recommended configuration interface does not expose
.Ev SFU_SERVER
shared-listener mode.
Use the media listener and port-range settings documented above.
.Sh AUTHENTICATION
Local host protection defaults are supplied by the package sample.
The root-only users file supplies
.Ev HOST_USERS
transiently at startup; the JWT secret is likewise kept out of this file.
See
.Xr kaigi 8 .
.Sh ENVIRONMENT OVERRIDES
The settings above are the recommended administration interface.
At startup Kaigi reads this file, expands references and maps the settings
to its application environment variables.
Environment entries that are already defined are not replaced and take
precedence over settings in this file.
For example,
.Ev SERVER_LISTEN_IP
overrides
.Ev WEB_LISTEN_IP ,
and
.Ev SFU_LISTEN_IP
overrides
.Ev MEDIA_LISTEN_IP .
Kaigi's application defaults are the final fallback.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "%%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/kaigi.conf.sample"
.It Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/kaigi.conf
Administrator-maintained deployment configuration.
.It Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/kaigi.conf.sample
Current package-provided example.
.It Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/site-assets/site.css
Administrator-maintained site stylesheet.
.It Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/users
Root-only local-user database.
.It Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/jwt-secret
Root-only generated JWT signing secret.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr rc.conf 5 ,
.Xr kaigi 8
.Sh PRIVACY POLICY
The site administrator defines the deployment's privacy policy.
The footer displays a Privacy Policy link only when one of the following is configured:
.Bl -tag -width PRIVACY_POLICY_NAME
.It Va PRIVACY_POLICY_NAME
Name of an administrator-owned HTML file below
.Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/site-assets .
For example,
.Dq privacy-private.html .
.It Va PRIVACY_POLICY_URL
Absolute URL of an externally hosted site privacy policy.
This takes precedence over
.Va PRIVACY_POLICY_NAME .
.El
If neither setting is present, no Privacy Policy link is displayed.
The package installs
.Pa privacy-private.html.sample
and
.Pa privacy-public.html.sample
as starting points only; administrators must review them against the site's actual operation before publication.
.Sh SITE PRESENTATION
The administrator-owned
.Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/site-assets/site.css
file is loaded after Kaigi's packaged stylesheets.  It may be used for local
presentation changes without modifying packaged application files.  The
installed sample includes commented examples for wide logos, custom static
landing/login backgrounds, and suppressing the animated hero illustration.
.Sh NETWORK PRIVACY
Kaigi does not configure a third-party STUN or TURN service by default.
WebRTC media connects directly to the configured mediasoup SFU.
A trusted TURN service may be configured explicitly when client network
conditions require one.
.Pp
Kaigi does not substitute a third-party service endpoint when an optional
service endpoint has not been configured.
.Sh LDAP AUTHENTICATION
.Va LDAP_ENABLED
accepts true or false and defaults to false.
LDAP support is included in Kaigi and disabled by default.
When enabled, settings are read from
.Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/ldap/ldap.conf .
When disabled, that file is neither read nor validated.
No inherited LDAP environment overrides are used.
All other LDAP variables belong in the separate file.
See
.Xr kaigi-ldap.conf 5 .
.Sh MEETING PROPERTIES
.Pa %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/meeting-properties.json
is a JSON object keyed by stable meeting key. Each entry contains an optional
names object (language codes mapped to display labels) and an optional
restrictions array. RP prohibits built-in recording and server recording uploads.
Names fall back from the exact language to its base language, English, then the key.
.Pp
A missing file or unlisted key imposes no restrictions and displays the key.
Unknown properties/codes, duplicate JSON keys, invalid labels, malformed JSON
and unreadable or unsafe existing files stop startup. Files must be regular,
non-symlink UTF-8 files no larger than 256 KiB and not writable by group or others.
This file grants no access. Labels for authorized meetings are provided only after login.
Restart after changes.
.Pp
Keys contain 1–128 printable Unicode characters, including spaces and punctuation.
Control/format/surrogate/unassigned characters and line/paragraph separators
are forbidden. Matching is exact; no normalization or case folding occurs.
.Sh CONNECTION DEADLINES
CONNECTION_INITIAL_TIMEOUT (15 seconds) bounds initial TCP/TLS and HTTP progress.
HTTP_HEADERS_TIMEOUT (30), HTTP_REQUEST_TIMEOUT (120), HTTP_IDLE_TIMEOUT (30),
and HTTP_KEEPALIVE_TIMEOUT (5) control HTTP lifetimes.
SOCKET_AUTH_TIMEOUT (30) closes Engine.IO transports that never authenticate.
These settings accept integers from 1 through 3600 seconds; zero is rejected.
Socket.IO handshakes require a valid, revocable session token.
Authenticated conference WebSockets are exempt from HTTP idle deadlines.
ENABLE_CHAT_TYPING defaults to false; enabling it shares typing activity, not draft text.
.Sh SECURITY EVENTS
JSON lines with component conference-security contain a UTC timestamp,
a stable event, verified direct peer IP and a fixed reason code.
No credentials, tokens or LDAP attributes are included in these event lines.
Kaigi uses the direct peer address for security attribution and ignores
client-supplied forwarded-address headers.
See HARDENING.md for Fail2ban integration on PF and IPFW hosts.
